  "Number": 34,
  "Question": "Let the roots of \\(x^2-3x+1=0\\) be \\(r\\) and \\(s.\\) Then the expression \\(r^2+s^2\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "a positive integer",
    "B": "a positive fraction greater than \\(1\\)",
    "C": "a positive fraction less than \\(1\\)",
    "D": "an irrational number",
    "E": "an imaginary number"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The expression treats the two roots symmetrically, so their sum and product can give its value without finding either root separately.\n\nSince \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are the roots of the monic quadratic,\n\\[\n(x-r)(x-s)=x^2-3x+1.\n\\]\nExpanding the left side gives \\(x^2-(r+s)x+rs\\). Matching coefficients therefore yields\n\\[\nr+s=3,\\qquad rs=1.\n\\]\nThe identity \\((r+s)^2=r^2+2rs+s^2\\) now gives\n\\[\nr^2+s^2=(r+s)^2-2rs\n=3^2-2\\cdot1=7.\n\\]\nThus the expression is the positive integer \\(7\\), choice A.",
